pmod{6}$ for $k \\geq 3$\n- $f(k) = 1$ if $k \\geq 4$ and $k \\not\\equiv 3 \\pmod{6}$\n\nWait, $k = 3$: $3 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{6}$, so $f(3) = 2$. \u2713\n\nLet me verify $k = 2$ with brute force. $x, y \\in \\{10, 11, \\ldots, 99\\}$.\n\nWe found: $x = 17, y = 34$ (from $b = 2$) and $x = 13, y = 52$ (from $b = 4$).\n\nLet me check if there are others. Actually, let me just verify these two and trust the analysis.\n\n$C(17, 34) = 1734$. $17 \\times 34 = 578$. $1734/578 = 3$. \u2713\n$C(13, 52) = 1352$. $13 \\times 52 = 676$. $1352/676 = 2$. \u2713\n\nOK so the answer to Part (1) is:\n$$f(k) = \\begin{cases} 5 & \\text{if } k = 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{if } k = 2 \\text{ or } k \\geq 3 \\text{ and } k \\equiv 3 \\pmod{6} \\\\ 1 & \\text{if } k \\geq 4 \\text{ and } k \\not\\equiv 3 \\pmod{6} \\end{cases}$$\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. $k = 2$: $2 \\not\\equiv 3 \\pmod{6}$, but $f(2) = 2$. So the formula needs a special case for $k = 2$.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the problem. Maybe I should double-check my claim that $a = 1$ is necessary.\n\nGoing back: $b(da \\cdot q - 1) = a \\cdot 10^n$ where $x = da, y = db, \\gcd(a,b) = 1, n = d(y)$.\n\nFrom this: $b \\mid a \\cdot 10^n$. Since $\\gcd(a, b) = 1$, we get $b \\mid 10^n$.\n\nThen $da \\cdot q - 1 = a \\cdot 10^n / b$.\n\nSo $da \\cdot q = a \\cdot 10^n / b + 1$.\n\nFor the RHS to be divisible by $da$ (so that $q$ is a positive integer):\n$da \\mid (a \\cdot 10^n / b + 1)$.\n\nSince $a \\mid a \\cdot 10^n / b$ (as $b \\mid 10^n$), we need $a \\mid 1$. So $a = 1$.\n\nYes, this confirms $a = 1$, i.e., $x = d = \\gcd(x, y)$, so $x \\mid y$.\n\nGreat, so my analysis is correct.\n\nNow for Part (2): We need to find all possible values of $C(x,y)/(xy) = q$.\n\nFrom our analysis, $q = (10^n/b + 1)/d$ where $x = d, y = db, b \\mid 10^n, \\gcd$-reduced.\n\nWait, more precisely: $n = d(y) = d(db)$. And $x = d$ must be a positive integer (no restriction on number of digits in Part 2).\n\nSo $q = (10^n + b)/(db)$ where $n = d(y) = d(d